.TH FORK 2
.SH NAME
rfork, spawn \- manipulate process resources
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libuser"
.PP
.nf
.B "rfork :: proc(flags: u64) -> i64"
.B "spawn :: proc(path: string, flags: u64, argv: []string = nil) -> i64"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Rfork
is Plan 9's fork.
.I Flags
is the logical OR of some subset of the
.B RF*
bits in
.BR sys/abi ,
and says which resources of the calling process (the parent) are shared
by a new process (the child), copied into it, or emptied for it.
If
.B RFPROC
is set, a new process is created; the value answered in the parent is the
child's pid, and the value answered in the child is zero, both at the
instruction after the call.
Without
.BR RFPROC ,
the flags act on the caller in place and the answer is zero.
.TP
.B RFPROC
make a child; without it the other flags change the caller instead
.TP
.B RFMEM
the child shares the parent's writable data and anonymous memory rather
than copying it; refused without
.B RFPROC
.TP
.B RFFDG
copy the descriptor table rather than share it
.TP
.B RFCFDG
give the child an empty descriptor table; mutually exclusive with
.B RFFDG
.TP
.B RFNAMEG
copy the name space rather than share it
.TP
.B RFCNAMEG
give the child an empty name space; mutually exclusive with
.B RFNAMEG
.TP
.B RFENVG
copy the environment rather than share it
.TP
.B RFCENVG
give the child an empty environment; mutually exclusive with
.B RFENVG
.TP
.B RFNOTEG
put the child in a note group of its own rather than its parent's; see
.IR notify (2)
.TP
.B RFNOWAIT
detach the child at birth: it has no parent to
.I wait
for it, and the kernel collects it when it ends; refused without
.B RFPROC
.TP
.B RFREND
put the caller (or, with
.BR RFPROC ,
the child) in a rendezvous group of its own; see
.IR rendezvous (2)
.PD
.PP
The text segment and every read-only segment are always shared, because
nothing can write them.
The stack is always copied: two processes returning through one stack
would unwind each other.
Descriptors and the name space default to
.IR shared ,
which is Plan 9's
.I fork
and not
.IR spawn 's.
A flag bit this kernel does not implement \(em Plan 9's
.B RFNOMNT
among them \(em is refused with
.BR EINVAL ,
rather than silently ignored.
.PP
.I Spawn
starts a program at
.I path
as a new process in one call, fork and exec together rather than
.I rfork
followed by
.IR exec .
.I Argv
is the new program's arguments, its own name first by convention: the
kernel adds nothing to it.
.I Flags
is the logical OR of
.BR abi.SPAWN_NS_COPY ,
which copies the caller's name space into the child instead of sharing
it,
.BR SPAWN_NS_CLEAN ,
which gives the child an empty name space, and
.BR SPAWN_FD_CLEAN ,
which gives the child an empty descriptor table instead of a copy of the
caller's.
Zero shares the name space and copies the descriptors, which is Plan 9's
default for a loaded program.
The answer is the new process's pid, the name
.I wait
takes.
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libuser/sys.odin
.br
.B kernel/user/rfork.odin
.br
.B kernel/user/spawn.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (2),
.IR exec (2),
.IR wait (2),
.IR notify (2),
.IR rendezvous (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Answers a pid, zero, or a negative
.B \-errno
as described above; see
.IR errstr (2).
.B EAGAIN
is a process table with no room for a child.
